# Copy to lab.env and edit. lab.env is gitignored; never commit it.
#   cp lab.env.example lab.env
# lab.env holds no secrets. It may also live elsewhere: export LAB_ENV_FILE=<path>.
# Save it with Linux (LF) line endings; Windows (CRLF) endings are stripped when
# loaded, but lib/preflight.sh warns about them.
# All values below are fake examples. Lines left commented use the default shown.

# --- vmrest API (account created with: vmrest.exe -C) ---
# Do NOT put the account in this file. Write it into the secrets file in your
# Windows user folder, %USERPROFILE%\.env (C:\Users\<you>\.env):
#   VMWS_USERNAME=example-api-user
#   VMWS_PASSWORD=change-me-not-a-real-password
# (VMWS_USER is accepted instead of VMWS_USERNAME.) Keep that file readable only
# by your Windows account. To use another file:
# LAB_SECRETS_FILE=/mnt/c/Users/example/.env
# VMWS_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:8697/api

# --- WSL side ---
# Private files (SSH key, inventory, known_hosts, seeds). Keep on the WSL filesystem.
# LAB_PRIVATE_DIR=$HOME/.config/wsl-vmware-lab
# The guest VM user is fixed: cloud-init creates "lab" (key login, passwordless
# sudo) in every clone; it is not a setting.
# The WSL user is not configured either: scripts run as whoever is logged in to WSL
# (the User of your SSH config entry for this PC).

# --- Windows side ---
# VMWARE_DIR="/mnt/c/Program Files/VMware/VMware Workstation"
# LAB_DHCP_LEASES=/mnt/c/ProgramData/VMware/vmnetdhcp.leases
# LAB_DHCP_CONF=/mnt/c/ProgramData/VMware/vmnetdhcp.conf
# LAB_NAT_VNET=VMnet8
# LAB_MGMT_VNET=VMnet1
# Empty or VMnet0 uses bridged; VMnet1-VMnet19 uses custom + ethernet2.vnet.
# Must differ from NAT and management. Inspect ./lab network before choosing.
# Changing this does not rebuild clones: up refuses existing LAN mismatches;
# follow the skill's reference/network.md offline VMX procedure.
# LAB_LAN_VNET=

# --- VMs ---
# Guest image of VMs without image = "..." in vms.tfvars: ubuntu-24.04
# (default), ubuntu-22.04 or debian-13. ./lab setup prepares one base per image
# in use (base/<image>/, VM "<image>-base"); changing a VM's image rebuilds it.
# (Debian publishes no signature for cloud images; only its checksum is checked.)
# LAB_IMAGE=ubuntu-24.04
# Windows folder for clones; each VM is <root>\<name>\<name>.vmx.
# Default: <Windows user profile>\Documents\Virtual Machines
# Must match VMware Workstation's default VM folder (Edit > Preferences >
# Workspace). Provider 2.0.1 ignores other paths, including subfolders, when
# cloning; a mismatch causes an inconsistent .path result and tainted resources.
# LAB_VM_ROOT_WIN='C:\Users\example\Documents\Virtual Machines'
# Several VMs (a whole system): copy vms.tfvars.example to vms.tfvars (gitignored).
# LAB_VMS_FILE=vms.tfvars
# Without vms.tfvars, one VM is created from these (Ansible group "demo"):
# LAB_CLONE_NAME=ubuntu24-demo
# LAB_CLONE_CPUS=2
# LAB_CLONE_MEMORY_MB=2048
# Playbook run by lib/configure.sh when none is given (default: the nginx example).
# LAB_PLAYBOOK=/home/example/my-system/site.yml
# Wall-clock readiness wait for the frozen powered-on set (./lab configure).
# LAB_GUEST_WAIT_SECONDS=300
# Graceful down waits for vmrest power-off confirmation, polling every 5 s.
# LAB_DOWN_WAIT_SECONDS=120
# Preview VM/state changes with up/down/destroy --dry-run (private scratch is used).
# All ./lab commands emit JSON on stdout and diagnostics on stderr.
